-- CISSP Domain 7 expansion, part D (final): objective 7.12 (testing disaster recovery plans - tabletop exercises, walkthrough tests, simulation tests, parallel tests, full-interruption tests, after-action reporting), 7.13 (business continuity planning - BCP development process, plan maintenance, integration with BIA/DRP; BCP-vs-DRP basics intentionally NOT restated, see ci-f-056), 7.14 (physical security - access control vestibules/mantraps, perimeter security, badge/biometric access systems, guards vs. technology tradeoffs), 7.15 (personnel safety and security - duress systems, travel security, workplace violence prevention, evacuation procedures).

insert into public.flashcards (id, cert, deck, domain, objective, front, back) values
-- 7.12 core (ci-f-1800..1811)
('ci-f-1800','cissp','core',7,'7.12',$$What is a tabletop exercise in DR/BC testing, and who typically participates?$$,$$A discussion-based test where key stakeholders (recovery team leads, department heads) talk through their roles and responses to a hypothetical disaster scenario around a table, without touching any live system or leaving their normal work location - the least disruptive test type, used to validate the plan's logic and build familiarity before more demanding tests.$$),
('ci-f-1801','cissp','core',7,'7.12',$$How does a walkthrough (structured walk-through) test differ from a tabletop exercise?$$,$$A walkthrough goes a step further than a tabletop discussion: participants procedurally step through the plan's specific tasks and checklists in detail - confirming each action, contact list, and resource reference is accurate and current - still without activating any system, but with much more granular verification than a tabletop's high-level talk-through.$$),
('ci-f-1802','cissp','core',7,'7.12',$$What happens during a simulation test, and what stays untouched?$$,$$A simulation test acts out a specific disaster scenario in as close to real time as practical - team members perform their actual recovery duties (e.g., actually calling the vendor, actually locating backup media) - but production systems and the primary site are never actually taken down or failed over; it tests behavior and coordination under realistic pressure without the risk of a real outage.$$),
('ci-f-1803','cissp','core',7,'7.12',$$What makes a parallel test more rigorous than a simulation test, while still keeping production safe?$$,$$A parallel test actually activates the alternate/recovery site and processes real or copied data on it, in parallel with the still-running primary site - proving the recovery systems genuinely work end-to-end - without disrupting live operations at all, since production keeps running throughout.$$),
('ci-f-1804','cissp','core',7,'7.12',$$What defines a full-interruption test, and why is it used sparingly?$$,$$A full-interruption test actually shuts down the primary site and fails real operations over to the alternate site, exactly as a genuine disaster would require. It is the only test that proves the plan works under true conditions, but the real business disruption if the failover fails means organizations run it rarely, and only after lower-risk tests have already built confidence.$$),
('ci-f-1805','cissp','core',7,'7.12',$$What is the primary objective a DR test is measured against, beyond simply "did the systems come back"?$$,$$Whether the actual recovery time and recovery point achieved during the test meet or beat the documented RTO and RPO targets for each in-scope system/process - a test that restores everything eventually, but far slower than the required RTO, has still failed its real objective even though "the system came back up."$$),
('ci-f-1806','cissp','core',7,'7.12',$$What should a formal after-action report capture following any DR/BC test?$$,$$What worked as planned, what didn't (gaps between documented procedures and what actually happened), specific corrective actions with named owners and target dates, and the actual metrics achieved (e.g., measured recovery time vs. the RTO target) - turning the test from a one-time event into documented input for the next plan revision.$$),
('ci-f-1807','cissp','core',7,'7.12',$$Why should DR/BC test scenarios be varied across test cycles rather than always rehearsing the same scenario?$$,$$Repeating an identical scenario (e.g., always "primary data center loses power") only proves the team can execute one memorized script, not that the underlying plan and skills generalize; rotating scenarios (cyberattack, key-person unavailability, supplier failure, regional disaster) exercises different decision points and surfaces gaps a familiar scenario would never expose.$$),
('ci-f-1808','cissp','core',7,'7.12',$$Why must a DR test's scope and success criteria be defined and agreed upon before the test begins, not afterward?$$,$$Without pre-agreed criteria (which systems are in scope, what RTO/RPO each is measured against, what counts as "pass"), a test's outcome becomes subjective and disputable after the fact, and stakeholders may walk away with contradictory conclusions about whether the plan actually works - defining success up front keeps the after-action review objective.$$),
('ci-f-1809','cissp','core',7,'7.12',$$What roles do a test coordinator and independent observers/evaluators play during a DR test?$$,$$The test coordinator plans the scenario, controls its pacing/injects (new complications introduced mid-test), and has authority to halt the test if real risk emerges. Independent observers/evaluators watch without participating, documenting what actually happened against the plan's documented steps - producing an objective record the absorbed participants cannot reliably self-report.$$),
('ci-f-1810','cissp','core',7,'7.12',$$How does NIST SP 800-34 categorize test/training/exercise (TT&E) activities for contingency plans?$$,$$NIST SP 800-34 groups TT&E activities into tests (verifying a specific plan element or capability actually works, such as a failover), training (teaching personnel the procedures and their roles), and exercises (simulating an incident to practice team response and decision-making) - the tabletop-through-full-interruption spectrum sits within this broader test/exercise category structure.$$),
('ci-f-1811','cissp','core',7,'7.12',$$How often should an organization's DR plan typically be tested, and what triggers an off-cycle test?$$,$$Most organizations test at least annually as a baseline, but any material change - a new critical system, a data center migration, significant staff turnover in recovery roles, or a failed/inconclusive prior test - should trigger an additional off-cycle test rather than waiting for the next scheduled cycle; an untested plan is only a hypothesis about what will work.$$),
-- 7.12 acronym (ci-a-580..582)
('ci-a-580','cissp','acronym',7,'7.12',$$TT&E$$,$$Test, Training, and Exercise (program) - the umbrella NIST SP 800-34 category covering all activities used to validate and rehearse a contingency plan, from tabletop discussions to full-interruption tests.$$),
('ci-a-581','cissp','acronym',7,'7.12',$$AAR$$,$$After-Action Report - the formal document produced after a DR/BC test or real incident, capturing what worked, what didn't, and assigned corrective actions with owners and due dates.$$),
('ci-a-582','cissp','acronym',7,'7.12',$$HSEEP$$,$$Homeland Security Exercise and Evaluation Program - a U.S. government-developed common methodology and terminology for designing, conducting, and evaluating exercises, widely referenced for structuring tabletop and functional exercises.$$),
-- 7.12 feynman (ci-y-370..372)
('ci-y-370','cissp','feynman',7,'7.12',$$Explain why a company might run a parallel test instead of jumping straight to a full-interruption test, even though the full-interruption test is more realistic.$$,$$Model answer: A full-interruption test actually shuts down live production and forces real business operations onto the recovery site - if the failover doesn't work as expected, the company suffers a genuine outage, exactly the disaster the plan was supposed to prevent. A parallel test gets most of the same proof: the recovery site is genuinely activated and processes real data, showing the technical capability actually works end-to-end, but production keeps running the whole time as a safety net. Organizations typically climb the ladder - tabletop, walkthrough, simulation, parallel - building confidence at each safer step, and only accept the real risk of a full interruption once the parallel test has already shown the recovery infrastructure functions correctly. Jumping straight to full interruption without that groundwork risks turning a planned test into an actual, unplanned disaster.$$),
('ci-y-371','cissp','feynman',7,'7.12',$$Explain why a DR test that successfully restores every system is not necessarily a "pass."$$,$$Model answer: The plan doesn't just promise "eventually recoverable" - each critical system has a documented RTO and RPO derived from the business impact analysis, representing the maximum downtime and data loss the business can actually tolerate. If a system rated for a 4-hour RTO takes 14 hours to restore during the test, the systems technically came back, but the test has revealed the plan cannot meet the business's real requirement - which is a failure needing a corrective action (faster runbooks, pre-staged resources, a different recovery strategy), even though nothing "broke" in the traditional sense. Success has to be measured against the target, not just against "did it eventually work."$$),
('ci-y-372','cissp','feynman',7,'7.12',$$Explain the value of introducing an unplanned "inject" (surprise complication) partway through a DR simulation test.$$,$$Model answer: A scripted scenario that unfolds exactly as written mostly tests whether people can follow a checklist, which is useful but limited. An inject - like announcing mid-test that the designated backup coordinator is unexpectedly "unavailable," or that the documented failover procedure just failed a step - forces the team to actually problem-solve and adapt under the same kind of uncertainty a real disaster would produce, rather than just executing a memorized script. It's the difference between testing whether the plan document is accurate and testing whether the team and plan together can actually handle the unexpected, which is closer to what a real disaster demands.$$),
-- 7.13 core (ci-f-1812..1823)
('ci-f-1812','cissp','core',7,'7.13',$$What are the phases of the BCP development process, from project initiation through ongoing maintenance?$$,$$Project initiation and scope definition -> Business Impact Analysis (identifying critical functions, MTD/RTO/RPO) -> recovery strategy development (selecting how each function will continue) -> plan design and documentation -> implementation (acquiring resources, building the actual plan) -> testing/training/exercising -> ongoing maintenance and continuous improvement, feeding back into the cycle as the business changes.$$),
('ci-f-1813','cissp','core',7,'7.13',$$How does the Business Impact Analysis (BIA) feed directly into BC recovery strategy development?$$,$$The BIA identifies which business functions are critical and quantifies each one's MTD/RTO/RPO and resource dependencies; recovery strategy development then selects a continuity approach - alternate site, manual workaround, cross-training, mutual aid agreement - for each function, specifically sized to meet the BIA's tolerance figures rather than applying one generic strategy everywhere.$$),
('ci-f-1814','cissp','core',7,'7.13',$$What triggers should prompt an off-cycle update to a BC plan, beyond its scheduled periodic review?$$,$$A significant organizational change (merger, acquisition, reorganization), a new critical business process or system, a major facility or vendor change, meaningful staff turnover in continuity roles, a failed or inconclusive test result, or lessons learned from an actual invocation of the plan - any of these can make the documented plan stale well before its next scheduled review date.$$),
('ci-f-1815','cissp','core',7,'7.13',$$What does "plan maintenance" mean beyond simply re-printing an updated document?$$,$$Maintenance is an ongoing program: assigning a plan owner accountable for currency, embedding plan updates into the organization's change management process so relevant changes automatically trigger a plan review, version-controlling every revision, and redistributing the current version to all plan holders - not a one-time edit that then sits untouched until the next audit.$$),
('ci-f-1816','cissp','core',7,'7.13',$$What is a Business Continuity Steering Committee, and why does BC planning typically need one?$$,$$A cross-functional group of senior stakeholders (business unit leaders, IT, legal, HR, facilities) who own and champion the BC program, approve the plan and its strategies, allocate the budget and resources it needs, and resolve conflicts between competing business-unit priorities - because a BC plan living only inside the security or IT team, without genuine business ownership, tends to be underfunded and disconnected from actual operational reality.$$),
('ci-f-1817','cissp','core',7,'7.13',$$What is succession planning, and why is it a BC planning concern rather than purely an HR function?$$,$$Succession planning identifies who is authorized and prepared to assume a critical role's responsibilities if the incumbent becomes unavailable during a disruption (illness, travel, resignation, incapacitation). It matters to BC planning because a plan that assumes every key decision-maker will be reachable and available during a crisis is itself a single point of failure - the plan needs a documented line of authority, not just a documented set of technical steps.$$),
('ci-f-1818','cissp','core',7,'7.13',$$What should a crisis communication plan specify, and who are its typical audiences?$$,$$Pre-approved notification templates, designated spokespeople, escalation and approval chains for public statements, and the specific channels/cadence used to reach each audience - employees and their families, customers, regulators, media, and shareholders - each of which needs different information, delivered through different channels, on a different timeline during a disruption.$$),
('ci-f-1819','cissp','core',7,'7.13',$$Why does an effective BC plan need to address non-IT business functions (HR, facilities, supply chain, customer service), not just technology recovery?$$,$$A disruption to a business function can occur without any IT failure at all - a facility becomes inaccessible, a key supplier can't deliver, staff can't reach the office - and a plan scoped only to "recover the servers" leaves the organization unable to actually resume operating even after its systems are back up; BC planning is deliberately broader than DR's IT-recovery scope for exactly this reason.$$),
('ci-f-1820','cissp','core',7,'7.13',$$What is a manual workaround / alternate business process, as a BC continuity strategy?$$,$$A documented, non-technology-dependent way to keep a critical function operating temporarily when its normal supporting system is unavailable - for example, processing orders on paper forms during a point-of-sale outage - trading efficiency for the ability to continue the function at all until the primary system is restored.$$),
('ci-f-1821','cissp','core',7,'7.13',$$Who should formally approve and sign off on a completed BC plan before it becomes the organization's official plan?$$,$$Senior management/executive leadership - approval is not a formality but the point at which leadership takes ownership of the plan's strategies, accepts the associated cost and residual risk, and commits the authority needed to actually execute the plan (declare a disruption, activate alternate sites, authorize spending) during a real event.$$),
('ci-f-1822','cissp','core',7,'7.13',$$What is version control's role in BC plan maintenance, beyond simple record-keeping?$$,$$It guarantees that everyone who might need to execute the plan during a crisis - potentially under stress, at 2 AM, from an unfamiliar location - is working from the current, accurate version rather than an outdated copy with stale contacts or superseded procedures; an uncontrolled plan with multiple circulating versions is a real cause of confusion during an actual invocation.$$),
('ci-f-1823','cissp','core',7,'7.13',$$How should DRP invocation authority relate to the broader BC plan's crisis-management structure?$$,$$DRP execution (the IT-focused recovery actions) should be triggered and overseen within the same overall incident/crisis command structure the BC plan establishes, rather than as an independent process - so IT recovery decisions (e.g., how long to wait before failing over) stay coordinated with the business-side decisions (e.g., how to communicate the outage to customers) happening in parallel.$$),
-- 7.13 acronym (ci-a-583..585)
('ci-a-583','cissp','acronym',7,'7.13',$$BCM$$,$$Business Continuity Management - the overall organizational discipline and ongoing program (governance, planning, testing, maintenance) of preparing for and responding to disruptions, of which the BCP document is one output.$$),
('ci-a-584','cissp','acronym',7,'7.13',$$BCMS$$,$$Business Continuity Management System - the formal, ISO 22301-defined system of policies, processes, and continual-improvement activities an organization uses to implement and govern its BCM program.$$),
('ci-a-585','cissp','acronym',7,'7.13',$$EOC$$,$$Emergency Operations Center - a designated physical or virtual location where the crisis-management/continuity team gathers to coordinate the response, communicate with stakeholders, and make decisions during an active disruption.$$),
-- 7.13 feynman (ci-y-373..375)
('ci-y-373','cissp','feynman',7,'7.13',$$Explain why a BIA has to happen before an organization can meaningfully choose its BC recovery strategies, rather than picking strategies first.$$,$$Model answer: Recovery strategies - a hot site, a manual workaround, cross-training staff at another location - all cost money and effort in proportion to how fast and complete they make recovery. Without a BIA telling you which functions are actually critical and what their real MTD/RTO/RPO tolerances are, you have no basis for deciding which functions deserve an expensive hot site versus which can tolerate a cheaper, slower manual workaround, or none at all. Picking strategies first risks either overspending on protecting a function nobody urgently needs back, or underprotecting one the business genuinely cannot survive losing for more than a few hours. The BIA supplies the requirements; the strategy has to be sized to fit them, not the other way around.$$),
('ci-y-374','cissp','feynman',7,'7.13',$$Explain why a BC plan that has never been updated since it was written is arguably worse than having no plan at all.$$,$$Model answer: A stale plan creates false confidence - management believes the organization is prepared, so they don't invest further attention or budget, while the actual document may reference a data center the company vacated two years ago, contacts who no longer work there, and a recovery strategy sized for a business a fraction of its current scale. During a real disruption, the response team following that plan wastes precious early hours discovering it doesn't match reality, time that could have been spent improvising a genuine response from scratch. Having no plan at least prompts people to think critically from the start; a wrong plan actively misdirects effort toward procedures that will fail.$$),
('ci-y-375','cissp','feynman',7,'7.13',$$Explain why BC planning needs an approved crisis communication plan even for functions and audiences that have nothing to do with IT.$$,$$Model answer: A disruption doesn't stay contained to the people directly executing recovery - employees' families want to know if they're safe, customers want to know if they'll get their orders, regulators may have mandatory notification windows, and reporters will publish something regardless of whether the company gives them accurate information. If there's no pre-approved plan for who says what to each audience, through which channel, on what timeline, the vacuum gets filled by rumor, panic, or an under-informed spokesperson accidentally creating legal or reputational damage worse than the original disruption. A crisis communication plan isn't about the technical recovery at all - it's about making sure the organization controls its own narrative and legal exposure while the technical team focuses on actually fixing the problem.$$),
-- 7.14 core (ci-f-1824..1835)
('ci-f-1824','cissp','core',7,'7.14',$$What is the difference between tailgating and piggybacking as physical social-engineering techniques, and how does an access control vestibule address both?$$,$$Tailgating is following an authorized person through a secured door without their knowledge or consent; piggybacking is doing so WITH an authorized person's knowing (if misguided) cooperation, such as someone holding the door open. An access control vestibule defeats both because its interlocking-door design physically allows only one verified individual through per authentication cycle - consent from the legitimate badge-holder can't override the mechanism the way it can override an unlocked door.$$),
('ci-f-1825','cissp','core',7,'7.14',$$What additional anti-piggybacking technology is sometimes added inside an access control vestibule beyond the interlocking doors themselves?$$,$$Weight sensors, optical/infrared beam counters, or cameras with people-counting analytics that detect if more than one person entered the vestibule per authentication event, triggering an alarm or refusing to open the second door even if a valid credential was presented - hardening the vestibule against a determined pair working together.$$),
('ci-f-1826','cissp','core',7,'7.14',$$What are the four conceptual layers of perimeter physical security, from outermost to innermost function?$$,$$Deterrence (signage, visible lighting/cameras discouraging an attempt), detection (CCTV, motion/intrusion sensors identifying an attempt in progress), delay (fencing, bollards, locks slowing an intruder down), and response (guards or law enforcement arriving before the intruder achieves their objective) - a facility's physical controls are meaningless without enough delay to let a response actually arrive in time.$$),
('ci-f-1827','cissp','core',7,'7.14',$$What do the continuous, standby, movable, and emergency categories of security lighting each provide?$$,$$Continuous lighting stays on at a fixed level all the time (the default deterrent/detection baseline); standby lighting activates automatically on a schedule or trigger (e.g., dusk-to-dawn); movable/trainable lighting can be redirected by a guard toward a specific area of concern; emergency lighting is backup power-driven lighting that activates only during a power failure, primarily for safe evacuation rather than surveillance.$$),
('ci-f-1828','cissp','core',7,'7.14',$$What is the security purpose of vehicle bollards around a building's entrance, distinct from a perimeter fence?$$,$$Bollards are short, sturdy posts specifically engineered (and often crash-rated) to stop a vehicle - whether a deliberate vehicle-ramming attack or an accidental crash - from reaching a building's entrance or a crowd, a threat a standard perimeter fence is not built to resist since fencing deters/delays pedestrian intrusion, not multi-ton vehicle impacts.$$),
('ci-f-1829','cissp','core',7,'7.14',$$What is the difference between a fixed CCTV camera and a PTZ (pan-tilt-zoom) camera in a physical security deployment?$$,$$A fixed camera continuously covers one unchanging field of view, providing predictable, always-on coverage of a specific area. A PTZ camera can be remotely or automatically panned, tilted, and zoomed to actively track a subject or investigate an alert across a wider area, trading that flexibility for the fact it can only actively watch one direction at a time.$$),
('ci-f-1830','cissp','core',7,'7.14',$$What are the two main roles CCTV serves in physical security, beyond simply "watching"?$$,$$A deterrent role (visible cameras discourage an attempt before it starts) and an evidentiary/investigative role (recorded footage supports incident investigation and prosecution after the fact) - CCTV is fundamentally a detective control, not a preventive one, since footage alone doesn't physically stop an intrusion in progress without a monitored response behind it.$$),
('ci-f-1831','cissp','core',7,'7.14',$$What is the difference between a proximity card and a smart card in badge-based access control?$$,$$A proximity card typically just broadcasts a static identifier read wirelessly at short range, functioning much like a simple key. A smart card contains an embedded microprocessor/chip capable of cryptographic operations (challenge-response, certificate-based authentication), allowing it to prove possession of a private key rather than merely transmit a static, more easily clonable identifier.$$),
('ci-f-1832','cissp','core',7,'7.14',$$What tradeoff does an organization accept when choosing a security guard over a purely technological control (badge reader, camera) for a given checkpoint?$$,$$A guard brings judgment, discretion, and adaptability - noticing something is "off" even without a defined rule, verifying identity against a photo, intervening physically - that fixed technology cannot; in exchange, the organization accepts higher ongoing cost, guard fatigue/inconsistency over long shifts, and susceptibility to social engineering or bribery that a well-configured automated system doesn't experience the same way.$$),
('ci-f-1833','cissp','core',7,'7.14',$$Why might an organization deploy security guards AND technology together at the same checkpoint rather than choosing one or the other?$$,$$The combination compensates for each approach's weak point: technology (badge readers, cameras) provides consistent, tireless, always-logged enforcement of the baseline rule, while a guard supplies the judgment to handle exceptions, verify identity against a photo, and physically respond to something the technology alone flags but cannot itself resolve - layered rather than either/or.$$),
('ci-f-1834','cissp','core',7,'7.14',$$What are the main strengths and weaknesses biometric access systems share as a category, regardless of the specific modality (fingerprint, iris, facial)?$$,$$Strength: biometrics are difficult to lose, forget, share, or lend to someone else the way a badge or PIN can be, tying access tightly to a specific individual. Weaknesses: a compromised biometric template generally can't be "reset" the way a password can, enrollment/scanning can be affected by injury or environmental conditions, and biometric data itself is highly sensitive personal information requiring strong protection if stored.$$),
('ci-f-1835','cissp','core',7,'7.14',$$What is the concept of concentric security zones (layered physical security), applied to a data center facility?$$,$$Protection is arranged in nested rings of increasing restriction, from the outer property perimeter, to the building perimeter, to a general office floor, to a restricted data center floor, down to an individual server cabinet - each ring requiring progressively stronger authentication, so that defeating the outer boundary alone doesn't grant access to the most sensitive asset at the center.$$),
-- 7.14 acronym (ci-a-586..588)
('ci-a-586','cissp','acronym',7,'7.14',$$CCTV$$,$$Closed-Circuit Television - a private video surveillance system whose signal is viewed/recorded on a limited set of monitors/recorders rather than being broadcast, used for both deterrence and post-incident investigation.$$),
('ci-a-587','cissp','acronym',7,'7.14',$$PTZ$$,$$Pan-Tilt-Zoom - a camera type that can be remotely or automatically repositioned and zoomed to actively track a subject or investigate an area, rather than covering one fixed field of view.$$),
('ci-a-588','cissp','acronym',7,'7.14',$$ACV$$,$$Access Control Vestibule - the current industry term (replacing the older "mantrap") for an interlocking double-door enclosure that admits only one verified person at a time.$$),
-- 7.14 feynman (ci-y-376..378)
('ci-y-376','cissp','feynman',7,'7.14',$$Explain why a facility might still employ human security guards at its main entrance even though it also has badge readers and cameras covering the same door.$$,$$Model answer: A badge reader only checks "does this credential validate," and a camera only records what happened - neither can exercise judgment. A guard can notice that the person presenting a valid badge doesn't match the photo on file, that someone is behaving nervously or is under duress, or that an unfamiliar delivery vehicle needs to be questioned before it's waved through - situations a fixed rule can't anticipate. In exchange, the organization accepts a guard is more expensive per hour of coverage than a camera, can get fatigued or distracted over a long shift, and, unlike a badge reader, could theoretically be socially engineered or bribed. Putting a guard alongside the technology rather than replacing it means the technology handles the routine, tireless enforcement while the guard handles the judgment calls the technology structurally cannot make.$$),
('ci-y-377','cissp','feynman',7,'7.14',$$Explain why an access control vestibule with interlocking doors defeats piggybacking even when the legitimate badge-holder is actively trying to let a second person in.$$,$$Model answer: A normal secured door is just a lock - once an authorized person opens it, nothing physically prevents a second person from walking through behind them if the first person is willing to hold it open. An access control vestibule changes the physical mechanism itself: the second door is engineered so it will not unlock until the first door has fully closed and a fresh authentication event has occurred for whoever remains inside. Even if the legitimate badge-holder wants to let a companion in, the vestibule doesn't have a "let someone else in too" mode - it only knows how to admit one verified person per cycle. That's the key difference between a mantrap/ACV and ordinary access control: the defense isn't relying on the badge-holder's judgment or policy compliance at all, it's built into the mechanics of the doors.$$),
('ci-y-378','cissp','feynman',7,'7.14',$$Explain why concentric (layered) physical security zones are more resilient than relying on one single, very strong perimeter control.$$,$$Model answer: A single strong perimeter, no matter how good, is still a single point of failure - if an attacker defeats it once (a stolen badge, a social-engineered guard, a cut fence), they gain access to everything inside with no further resistance. Concentric zones assume any one layer might eventually fail, and require an attacker to defeat several independent controls in sequence - the outer gate, the building entrance, the restricted floor, the server cabinet lock - each one potentially different in nature (a badge, a biometric scan, an escort requirement) so a technique that beats one layer doesn't automatically beat the next. It trades the simplicity of "one very strong wall" for the resilience of "several different walls," which also buys time for detection and response between layers.$$),
-- 7.15 core (ci-f-1836..1847)
('ci-f-1836','cissp','core',7,'7.15',$$What is a duress alarm/duress system, and how is it typically triggered without alerting an attacker?$$,$$A duress system lets a person under coercion silently signal for help - for example, entering a specific "duress code" instead of their real PIN at an access panel, or pressing a hidden panic button - which appears to function normally to anyone watching but actually triggers a silent alert to security/law enforcement, rather than an obvious alarm that could escalate the immediate danger.$$),
('ci-f-1837','cissp','core',7,'7.15',$$Why is a duress code deliberately designed to look like a normal, valid credential rather than an obviously different one?$$,$$If entering a duress code visibly differed from a normal login (an extra button press, a distinct prompt), an attacker watching the process could recognize it and react violently before help arrives; the code is built to be functionally and visibly indistinguishable from ordinary access so the person under threat can raise a silent alert without tipping off whoever is coercing them.$$),
('ci-f-1838','cissp','core',7,'7.15',$$What should a pre-travel risk assessment for an employee traveling to a higher-risk destination cover?$$,$$Country/region-specific threat intelligence (crime, political instability, health risks), required documentation and local laws, communication and check-in protocols during the trip, an emergency contact and evacuation plan specific to that location, and often a security briefing covering local customs and situational-awareness practices before departure.$$),
('ci-f-1839','cissp','core',7,'7.15',$$Why do security teams often recommend "loaner"/burner devices for employees traveling to certain high-risk or high-surveillance countries, instead of their normal corporate laptop and phone?$$,$$A device carrying the organization's full email history, VPN credentials, and stored data becomes a much higher-value target for search, seizure, or covert compromise at a border crossing or in a high-surveillance environment; a wiped loaner device with minimal data and access limits what an adversary can obtain even if the device is inspected, copied, or compromised while abroad.$$),
('ci-f-1840','cissp','core',7,'7.15',$$What does Kidnap and Ransom (K&R) insurance typically cover, and for which employees is it most relevant?$$,$$K&R insurance covers costs related to a kidnapping, extortion, or hostage situation - negotiation consultants, ransom reimbursement, crisis response, and related liability - and is typically carried for executives, and employees who travel to or work in higher-risk regions where this threat is a realistic operational concern.$$),
('ci-f-1841','cissp','core',7,'7.15',$$What are the four commonly referenced categories (Types I-IV) of workplace violence?$$,$$Type I - violence by a stranger with no legitimate business at the workplace (e.g., a robbery); Type II - violence by a customer or client the organization serves; Type III - violence between coworkers ("worker-on-worker"); Type IV - violence from a personal relationship (e.g., domestic violence) that spills over into the workplace.$$),
('ci-f-1842','cissp','core',7,'7.15',$$What is a workplace threat assessment team, and what does it typically evaluate?$$,$$A cross-functional group (often HR, security, legal, and sometimes an employee assistance/mental health professional) that evaluates reports of concerning behavior - explicit threats, escalating hostility, warning signs of potential violence - to determine the actual risk level and appropriate intervention, rather than leaving that judgment to any single manager acting alone.$$),
('ci-f-1843','cissp','core',7,'7.15',$$What role does an Employee Assistance Program (EAP) play in workplace violence prevention?$$,$$An EAP provides confidential counseling and support resources (mental health, financial, substance abuse, conflict resolution) that employees can access before a personal or workplace stressor escalates into a crisis - functioning as an early, supportive intervention point rather than a purely reactive/disciplinary response after concerning behavior has already occurred.$$),
('ci-f-1844','cissp','core',7,'7.15',$$What is the difference between an evacuation and a shelter-in-place order during a physical emergency?$$,$$Evacuation moves occupants out of the building/area entirely, appropriate when the threat is inside or the building itself is unsafe (fire, gas leak, structural damage). Shelter-in-place keeps occupants inside a secured location, appropriate when the danger is outside (severe weather, an active threat in the vicinity) and moving people outdoors would expose them to greater risk than staying put.$$),
('ci-f-1845','cissp','core',7,'7.15',$$What is the role of a designated floor warden/evacuation warden during a building evacuation?$$,$$A trained employee assigned to a specific floor or zone who confirms the area has been fully cleared during an evacuation, assists anyone needing help (mobility-impaired occupants, visitors unfamiliar with the exits), directs people to the correct route and assembly point, and reports the area's status to incident command - providing accountability beyond simply "the alarm sounded."$$),
('ci-f-1846','cissp','core',7,'7.15',$$What is the purpose of a designated assembly point (muster point) after an evacuation?$$,$$A pre-identified safe location outside the building where evacuated occupants gather so a headcount can be taken against expected occupancy, letting responders quickly determine whether anyone is unaccounted for and may still be inside - without a muster point, an evacuation provides no way to confirm everyone actually got out safely.$$),
('ci-f-1847','cissp','core',7,'7.15',$$Why should personnel-safety planning treat life safety as the single highest priority, taking precedence even over protecting data or facilities?$$,$$Every other security objective - protecting data, systems, or property - only matters to an organization that still has people to run it; virtually every recognized security and life-safety framework establishes that when a control (like a locked door) would trade human safety for asset protection during an emergency, the correct default is always to preserve life first and address the resulting security or financial exposure afterward.$$),
-- 7.15 acronym (ci-a-589..591)
('ci-a-589','cissp','acronym',7,'7.15',$$EAP$$,$$Employee Assistance Program - confidential employer-sponsored counseling and support services (mental health, substance abuse, financial, conflict resolution) that can serve as an early intervention point before a personal stressor escalates into a workplace safety incident.$$),
('ci-a-590','cissp','acronym',7,'7.15',$$K&R$$,$$Kidnap and Ransom (insurance) - a policy covering negotiation, ransom reimbursement, and crisis-response costs, typically carried for executives and employees traveling to or working in higher-risk regions.$$),
('ci-a-591','cissp','acronym',7,'7.15',$$OSHA$$,$$Occupational Safety and Health Administration - the U.S. federal agency setting and enforcing workplace safety standards, relevant to an organization's legal duty of care for employee physical safety, including violence-prevention obligations.$$),
-- 7.15 feynman (ci-y-379..381)
('ci-y-379','cissp','feynman',7,'7.15',$$Explain why a duress code needs to be something the person under threat can enter without the attacker noticing anything unusual, and why this makes duress systems harder to design well than a normal panic button.$$,$$Model answer: A conspicuous panic button worn openly or a visibly different unlock sequence is fine when no one is watching, but a duress scenario specifically involves an attacker standing right there, forcing the person to open a door or access an account - reaching for an obvious separate button, or visibly entering something different from a normal PIN, risks provoking immediate violence if the attacker notices. A well-designed duress code (like a reversed PIN, or one specific extra digit) has to look and feel exactly like ordinary access from the outside, while still triggering a genuinely silent alert behind the scenes - which is a harder design problem than a simple panic button, because it has to fool an attacker who is actively watching the exact action being performed, not just be present as a backup option.$$),
('ci-y-380','cissp','feynman',7,'7.15',$$Explain why a company might issue a wiped "burner" laptop to an executive traveling to a high-surveillance country instead of just telling them to be careful with their normal laptop.$$,$$Model answer: Being "careful" doesn't change what's actually stored on the device - the corporate laptop typically holds cached email, saved VPN credentials, browser history, and access to internal systems, all of which becomes exposed the moment the device is searched, imaged, or compromised, regardless of how careful the traveler personally is. A wiped loaner device with minimal data and no standing access to sensitive systems means that even in the worst case - border seizure, hotel room compromise, forced inspection - there is very little of value for an adversary to actually obtain. The mitigation targets what the device contains, not the traveler's individual vigilance, because vigilance alone can't stop a legally compelled search or a covert compromise the traveler never even notices.$$),
('ci-y-381','cissp','feynman',7,'7.15',$$Explain why an evacuation plan needs a designated muster point and headcount process, not just an alarm and clear exits.$$,$$Model answer: Sounding an alarm and having clear exits gets people physically out of a building, but it tells responders nothing about whether everyone actually made it out. Without a pre-identified assembly point and a way to count who's present against who's expected to be in the building, first responders have no way to know if someone is still trapped inside versus simply already gone home, stuck in traffic, or standing on the wrong side of the building. That uncertainty is dangerous in both directions: responders might spend critical time searching a building that's actually already fully clear, or fail to realize someone genuinely needs rescue because no one confirmed they were missing. The muster point and headcount turn "everyone should be out" into a verified fact.$$);

insert into public.questions (id, cert, domain, objective, qtype, difficulty, stem, choices, answer, explanation) values
-- 7.12 test disaster recovery plans (ci-q-1800..1814)
('ci-q-1800','cissp',7,'7.12','mcq',1,$q$Which DR test type involves only a discussion-based review of the plan's steps by key stakeholders, without activating any system or leaving their normal work location?$q$,$q$["Tabletop exercise","Parallel test","Full-interruption test","Simulation test"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A tabletop exercise is purely discussion-based - stakeholders talk through the plan and their roles around a table, the least disruptive and least expensive way to validate a plan's logic before committing to more demanding tests.$q$),
('ci-q-1801','cissp',7,'7.12','mcq',2,$q$A DR test activates the alternate recovery site and processes real transaction data on it, while the primary production site continues operating normally and unaffected throughout. What type of test is this?$q$,$q$["Parallel test","Full-interruption test","Tabletop exercise","Checklist review"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A parallel test genuinely activates the alternate site and proves its recovery capability with real processing, but keeps production running as a safety net the entire time - distinguishing it from a full-interruption test, which actually shuts production down.$q$),
('ci-q-1802','cissp',7,'7.12','mcq',2,$q$A DR coordinator announces a fabricated scenario ("the primary data center has just lost power") and the recovery team responds as though it is truly happening - actually phoning the notification tree, actually locating backup media - all without taking any live production system offline. Which test type is this?$q$,$q$["A simulation test","A walkthrough (structured walk-through) test","A parallel test","A full-interruption test"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A simulation test acts out a specific scenario in near-real time, with the team performing genuine recovery duties, distinguishing it from a walkthrough (which verifies procedural accuracy rather than acting out a scenario) and from parallel/full-interruption tests (which actually involve live systems).$q$),
('ci-q-1803','cissp',7,'7.12','mcq',3,$q$A test coordinator, near the midpoint of a simulation test, unexpectedly announces that the designated recovery site manager is "unreachable," forcing the team to adapt on the fly. What is this technique called, and why is it used?$q$,$q$["An inject - to test the team's ability to adapt to unplanned complications, not just follow a script","A full-interruption escalation - to increase legal liability","A checklist deviation - a scoring penalty applied automatically","A parallel failover - to test alternate-site capacity"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$An inject is a deliberately introduced, unscripted complication designed to test genuine adaptability and decision-making under pressure, going beyond what a purely scripted scenario can reveal about the team's real readiness.$q$),
('ci-q-1804','cissp',7,'7.12','mcq',2,$q$Which DR test type carries the HIGHEST risk of a genuine, unplanned business disruption if the recovery procedure fails during the test itself?$q$,$q$["Full-interruption test","Tabletop exercise","Walkthrough test","Simulation test"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A full-interruption test actually shuts down the primary site and fails real operations over to the alternate site - if the failover doesn't work as planned, the organization suffers a genuine, live outage, unlike the other test types where production either isn't touched or keeps running throughout.$q$),
('ci-q-1805','cissp',7,'7.12','ordering',3,$q$Order the following DR test types from LEAST disruptive/risky to MOST disruptive/risky to live production operations.$q$,$q$["Tabletop exercise","Walkthrough test","Simulation test","Parallel test","Full-interruption test"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3,4]$q$::jsonb,$q$Each step up this ladder trades lower risk and cost for higher realism: a tabletop is pure discussion, a walkthrough adds detailed procedural verification, a simulation acts out a scenario without touching production, a parallel test activates the alternate site alongside still-running production, and a full-interruption test actually shuts production down - the only type that proves the plan works under true conditions, at the highest risk.$q$),
('ci-q-1806','cissp',7,'7.12','mcq',1,$q$What is the primary purpose of a DR/BC test's after-action report (AAR)?$q$,$q$["To document what worked, what didn't, and assign specific corrective actions with owners and deadlines for the next plan revision","To determine which employees should be disciplined for mistakes made during the test","To calculate the exact monetary cost of the test for budgeting purposes only","To replace the need for any future testing once corrective actions are listed"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$An AAR turns a test from a one-time event into documented, actionable input for improving the plan - capturing gaps, assigning ownership of fixes, and recording the metrics actually achieved, not assigning blame or eliminating the need for future tests.$q$),
('ci-q-1807','cissp',7,'7.12','mcq',2,$q$A DR test's success criteria specify that the order-processing system must be restored within 4 hours to meet its documented RTO. During the test, the system is fully restored, but only after 11 hours. How should this result be characterized?$q$,$q$["A failed test result, because the achieved recovery time did not meet the documented RTO even though the system was eventually restored","A passed test result, because every system was eventually restored regardless of duration","An inconclusive result, because RTOs apply only to real disasters, not tests","A passed test result, since 11 hours is within the MTD by definition"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Success is measured against the pre-agreed RTO, not merely "did it eventually come back." Missing the RTO by that margin is a genuine gap the after-action report needs to capture and address, even though no system technically failed to restore.$q$),
('ci-q-1808','cissp',7,'7.12','multi',2,$q$Select the TWO reasons an organization should vary its DR/BC test scenarios across successive test cycles rather than always rehearsing the identical scenario.$q$,$q$["A repeated identical scenario mainly proves the team can execute a memorized script, not that skills generalize","Varying scenarios (cyberattack, key-person unavailability, supplier failure) exercises different decision points and surfaces different gaps","Regulators require a new scenario be invented for every single test by law","Varying the scenario eliminates the need for an after-action report"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$Rotating scenarios exercises genuinely different decision points and reveals gaps a familiar, memorized scenario would never expose; there is no universal legal mandate requiring a new scenario each time, and an after-action report is still needed regardless of scenario choice.$q$),
('ci-q-1809','cissp',7,'7.12','mcq',1,$q$Which document/framework provides a common U.S. government-developed methodology and terminology widely referenced for designing and evaluating tabletop and functional exercises?$q$,$q$["HSEEP (Homeland Security Exercise and Evaluation Program)","GDPR","FRCP","COBIT"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$HSEEP standardizes exercise design, conduct, and evaluation terminology and methodology, and is widely referenced when structuring tabletop and functional exercises. GDPR, FRCP, and COBIT address privacy law, e-discovery procedure, and IT governance respectively - unrelated to exercise design.$q$),
('ci-q-1810','cissp',7,'7.12','mcq',2,$q$What is the defining difference between a test coordinator and an independent observer/evaluator during a DR exercise?$q$,$q$["The coordinator plans and controls the scenario's pacing and can halt the test; the observer watches without participating and objectively documents what actually happened","The coordinator only watches while the observer actively performs recovery tasks","There is no meaningful difference - both roles are interchangeable","The observer's only job is to time how long the test takes, nothing else"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$The coordinator actively controls the exercise (scenario, pacing, injects, and the authority to halt it if real risk emerges), while the observer/evaluator stays outside the action to produce an objective record - something participants absorbed in executing the plan cannot reliably self-report.$q$),
('ci-q-1811','cissp',7,'7.12','matching',3,$q$Match each DR test type to the description that best fits it.$q$,$q${"left":["Tabletop exercise","Walkthrough test","Simulation test","Parallel test","Full-interruption test"],"right":["An acted-out scenario performed in real time by the team, without any actual failover","Production actually shut down and operations genuinely failed over to the alternate site","Discussion-based review of the plan and roles, no systems touched","Alternate site actually activated and processes real data alongside still-running production","Detailed procedural verification of checklists and contact information, no live systems touched"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[2,4,0,3,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$Tabletop is discussion-only; walkthrough verifies procedural accuracy in detail; simulation acts out a scenario in real time without failover; parallel activates the alternate site alongside still-running production; full-interruption actually shuts production down and fails over for real - each step increasing in both realism and risk.$q$),
('ci-q-1812','cissp',7,'7.12','mcq',3,$q$An organization has never conducted anything beyond tabletop exercises for its DR plan, citing cost concerns. What is the most significant risk of relying solely on tabletop-level testing?$q$,$q$["A tabletop exercise validates the plan's logic and team familiarity but never proves the technical recovery capability (backups, alternate-site systems, failover mechanics) actually functions","Tabletop exercises are more expensive than parallel tests, making cost concerns misplaced","Tabletop exercises legally satisfy all DR testing compliance obligations in every jurisdiction","Tabletop exercises automatically update the BIA's RTO/RPO figures"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Tabletop exercises are valuable for validating logic and familiarity, but because no system is ever touched, they cannot demonstrate that backups actually restore, alternate-site infrastructure actually works, or failover mechanics actually function - only tests further up the ladder (simulation, parallel, full-interruption) can prove that.$q$),
('ci-q-1813','cissp',7,'7.12','mcq',1,$q$Per NIST SP 800-34's contingency-plan test/training/exercise (TT&E) framework, which category specifically focuses on teaching personnel the procedures and their individual roles, rather than verifying a technical capability or practicing team decision-making?$q$,$q$["Training","Testing","Exercising","Auditing"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$NIST SP 800-34's TT&E model separates training (teaching personnel procedures and roles) from testing (verifying a specific capability actually works) and exercising (practicing team response and decision-making) - each addresses a distinct readiness gap.$q$),
('ci-q-1814','cissp',7,'7.12','mcq',2,$q$A recently tested DR plan revealed that the documented emergency contact list still listed a vendor account manager who left the company eight months ago. What does this finding demonstrate about the value of testing, independent of whether any system failed over successfully?$q$,$q$["Testing surfaces plan-maintenance gaps (stale data) even when the technical recovery mechanics work correctly","The test result is invalid and must be discarded entirely","This finding is irrelevant to DR testing and belongs solely to HR","It proves the vendor's contract has been breached"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Testing has value beyond proving systems recover - it also exposes plan-maintenance gaps like stale contact information, which could genuinely delay a real recovery even if every technical component works flawlessly, feeding directly into the after-action report's corrective actions.$q$),
-- 7.13 business continuity planning (ci-q-1815..1828)
('ci-q-1815','cissp',7,'7.13','mcq',1,$q$Which phase of the BCP development process directly precedes and provides the required input for selecting a recovery strategy for each critical business function?$q$,$q$["Business Impact Analysis","Plan testing","Plan maintenance","Crisis communication drafting"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$The BIA identifies which functions are critical and quantifies their MTD/RTO/RPO, giving recovery strategy development the requirements it needs to size an appropriate continuity approach for each function.$q$),
('ci-q-1816','cissp',7,'7.13','mcq',2,$q$An organization completes a merger with another company, roughly doubling its critical business processes and locations. What should this trigger regarding the existing BC plan?$q$,$q$["An off-cycle plan update, since the merger materially changed the scope the plan needs to cover, well before the next scheduled review","No action, since BC plans are only updated on their fixed annual schedule","Immediate plan deletion, since a merged company always needs an entirely new plan from scratch","Automatic extension of the RTOs for all affected functions"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A significant organizational change like a merger is a textbook trigger for an off-cycle update - waiting for the next scheduled review would leave the plan materially out of date for a potentially long stretch.$q$),
('ci-q-1817','cissp',7,'7.13','mcq',2,$q$What is the primary purpose of a Business Continuity Steering Committee composed of senior business-unit leaders?$q$,$q$["To provide business ownership, budget, and authority for the BC program rather than leaving it isolated inside IT/security","To perform the technical failover of IT systems during a disaster","To replace the need for a formal Business Impact Analysis","To serve solely as the organization's crisis media spokespeople"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$The steering committee gives the BC program genuine business ownership, budget, and cross-functional authority - a plan that lives only inside IT/security without this backing tends to be underfunded and disconnected from real operational priorities.$q$),
('ci-q-1818','cissp',7,'7.13','mcq',1,$q$What does succession planning address within a BC plan?$q$,$q$["Who is authorized and prepared to take over a critical role's responsibilities if the incumbent is unavailable during a disruption","Which vendor will be selected to host the alternate recovery site","How data backups are rotated and retained","Which encryption algorithm protects data at the recovery site"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Succession planning ensures a documented line of authority exists for critical decision-making roles, so the plan doesn't collapse simply because one key person happens to be unreachable during the disruption.$q$),
('ci-q-1819','cissp',7,'7.13','mcq',2,$q$A company's BC plan for its order-fulfillment function specifies that staff will revert to processing orders on paper forms if the point-of-sale system becomes unavailable. What type of continuity strategy is this?$q$,$q$["A manual workaround / alternate business process","A hot site strategy","A parallel test","Data remanence mitigation"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A manual workaround keeps a critical function running through a non-technology-dependent method, trading efficiency for continuity until the primary system is restored - distinct from a technical recovery-site strategy like a hot site.$q$),
('ci-q-1820','cissp',7,'7.13','mcq',2,$q$Why does an effective BC plan need to cover non-IT functions such as facilities access, supply chain, and customer service, not only technology recovery?$q$,$q$["A business function can be disrupted without any IT failure at all - such as an inaccessible facility or a failed supplier - so a technology-only plan can't actually resume the function","Non-IT functions never actually experience disruptions in practice","Covering non-IT functions is legally required only for publicly traded companies","IT recovery automatically resolves any non-IT disruption as a side effect"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$BC planning is deliberately broader than DR's IT-focused scope precisely because a facility closure, a failed supplier, or unreachable staff can halt a business function with zero IT systems ever going down.$q$),
('ci-q-1821','cissp',7,'7.13','mcq',3,$q$A completed BC plan documents strong recovery strategies for every critical function, but it has never been formally approved by executive leadership. What is the most significant practical risk of leaving the plan in this state?$q$,$q$["Without executive sign-off, the organization hasn't actually committed the authority, budget, or acceptance of residual risk needed to execute the plan's strategies during a real disruption","The plan cannot legally be tested until it is approved","Unapproved plans are automatically void after 90 days by regulation","Approval only affects which font the plan document uses"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Executive approval is the point where leadership actually commits the organization's authority and budget to the plan's strategies - without it, the plan may look complete on paper but lacks the institutional backing needed to actually be executed during a real event.$q$),
('ci-q-1822','cissp',7,'7.13','multi',2,$q$Select the TWO items that belong in a documented crisis communication plan.$q$,$q$["Pre-approved notification templates and designated spokespeople for each audience","Escalation and approval chains for public statements","The organization's full annual IT security budget","The complete source code of every critical application"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$A crisis communication plan focuses on who says what, to which audience, through which channel, and who must approve it first - the IT budget and application source code have no place in this document.$q$),
('ci-q-1823','cissp',7,'7.13','mcq',1,$q$What is the defining feature of a Business Continuity Management System (BCMS) as described in ISO 22301?$q$,$q$["A formal, continually improved system of policies and processes for governing an organization's business continuity program, not just a single static plan document","A single spreadsheet tracking RTOs for every application","A backup software product used exclusively for database replication","A physical alternate site building"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A BCMS is a governance system - policies, processes, and continual improvement - not a single artifact; the BC plan document itself is just one output of a properly operating BCMS.$q$),
('ci-q-1824','cissp',7,'7.13','mcq',2,$q$A BC plan's document control log shows three different versions circulating among department heads, with no clear indication of which is current. What risk does this most directly create?$q$,$q$["During an actual disruption, responders may execute outdated procedures or contact stale personnel, creating confusion at the exact moment clarity matters most","The plan automatically becomes legally unenforceable","Version control issues only affect DR plans, never BC plans","This has no practical impact as long as one copy is eventually correct"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Uncontrolled plan versions are a real, documented cause of confusion during actual invocations - responders working from a stale copy may follow superseded steps or call people who are no longer the right contact.$q$),
('ci-q-1825','cissp',7,'7.13','ordering',3,$q$Order the following BC plan development activities from first to last.$q$,$q$["Define project scope and secure executive sponsorship","Conduct the Business Impact Analysis","Select and design recovery strategies for critical functions","Document, test, and formally approve the plan","Maintain and update the plan on an ongoing basis"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3,4]$q$::jsonb,$q$The BCP lifecycle begins with scoping and sponsorship, then the BIA identifies what's critical and how tolerant it is of downtime, strategies are then selected and sized to those requirements, the plan is documented/tested/approved, and finally maintained on an ongoing basis as the organization changes.$q$),
('ci-q-1826','cissp',7,'7.13','mcq',3,$q$An organization's DRP team decides independently, without coordinating with the broader BC crisis-management structure, exactly when to fail over a critical application to the recovery site. What is the primary risk of this lack of coordination?$q$,$q$["The IT recovery decision may become misaligned with business-side decisions happening in parallel, such as customer communication timing or which functions matter most right now","DRP teams are legally required to act independently of any BC structure","This approach guarantees a faster recovery time in every case","It eliminates the need for a documented DRP entirely"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$DRP execution should sit within the same overall crisis-command structure the BC plan establishes, so a purely technical failover decision doesn't work against business-side decisions - like customer messaging timing or prioritization - being made in parallel.$q$),
('ci-q-1827','cissp',7,'7.13','mcq',1,$q$What best distinguishes "plan maintenance" from a one-time plan edit?$q$,$q$["Maintenance is an ongoing program - an assigned owner, change-management integration, version control, and redistribution - not a single edit that then sits untouched","Maintenance means the plan is rewritten completely from scratch every year","Maintenance is performed exclusively by external auditors","Maintenance only applies after a real disaster has occurred"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Maintenance is a continuous program with an accountable owner, integration into change management so relevant events trigger review, version control, and redistribution - not a rewrite-from-scratch cycle or a purely post-disaster activity.$q$),
('ci-q-1828','cissp',7,'7.13','matching',3,$q$Match each BC planning role/body to its primary responsibility.$q$,$q${"left":["Business Continuity Steering Committee","Plan owner","Executive leadership","Crisis communication spokesperson"],"right":["Delivers pre-approved messaging to a designated audience during a disruption","Provides business ownership, budget, and authority for the overall BC program","Formally approves the plan, committing the organization's authority and accepted risk","Is accountable for keeping the plan's content current and version-controlled"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[1,3,2,0]$q$::jsonb,$q$The steering committee provides business ownership and authority; the plan owner is accountable for keeping content current; executive leadership formally approves the plan and commits organizational authority; the spokesperson delivers pre-approved messaging to a designated audience - four distinct responsibilities that together sustain the BC program.$q$),
-- 7.14 physical security (ci-q-1829..1843)
('ci-q-1829','cissp',7,'7.14','mcq',1,$q$What is the modern industry term the 2024 CISSP outline uses for what was traditionally called a "mantrap"?$q$,$q$["Access control vestibule","Perimeter interlock","Security turnstile","Detection corridor"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$"Access control vestibule" is the current terminology for the interlocking double-door enclosure historically called a mantrap; both describe the same physical control admitting one verified person per authentication cycle.$q$),
('ci-q-1830','cissp',7,'7.14','mcq',2,$q$An unauthorized person follows closely behind an employee through a secured door, without the employee's knowledge, before the door closes. What is this specific technique called?$q$,$q$["Tailgating","Piggybacking","Shoulder surfing","Pretexting"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Tailgating specifically describes following an authorized person through a secured door without their knowledge or consent - distinguished from piggybacking, where the authorized person knowingly (if misguidedly) cooperates.$q$),
('ci-q-1831','cissp',7,'7.14','mcq',2,$q$An employee, believing they are being helpful, holds a secured door open for someone they don't recognize who claims to have forgotten their badge. What is this specific technique called?$q$,$q$["Piggybacking","Tailgating","Vishing","Baiting"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Piggybacking involves the authorized person's knowing cooperation, even if well-intentioned - distinguishing it from tailgating, which happens without the authorized person's awareness or consent.$q$),
('ci-q-1832','cissp',7,'7.14','mcq',2,$q$Why does an access control vestibule defeat piggybacking even when the legitimate badge-holder is actively trying to let a second person through?$q$,$q$["The vestibule's interlocking doors mechanically permit only one verified person per authentication cycle, regardless of the badge-holder's intent or cooperation","The vestibule automatically calls a security guard whenever two people are detected","The vestibule only opens for pre-registered visitor groups","Piggybacking is not actually possible in any facility, making the vestibule unnecessary"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$The defense is built into the mechanics, not into policy compliance - the second door simply will not open for a second person within the same authentication cycle, regardless of what the legitimate badge-holder intends or wants.$q$),
('ci-q-1833','cissp',7,'7.14','mcq',3,$q$A facility's access control vestibule uses interlocking doors alone, with no additional sensors. Two colleagues, both holding valid badges, deliberately enter together in a single cycle by having the first badge in and then quickly pulling the second person in before the first door closes. What additional control would most directly detect this specific scenario?$q$,$q$["Weight sensors or optical people-counting sensors inside the vestibule","A taller perimeter fence","A higher-resolution badge photo","Emergency lighting"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Weight or optical people-counting sensors specifically detect when more than one person has entered the vestibule per authentication cycle, closing the gap that a purely mechanical interlocking-door design alone can't catch when two people move together quickly.$q$),
('ci-q-1834','cissp',7,'7.14','mcq',1,$q$Which physical security layer does a standard chain-link perimeter fence, without integrated sensors, primarily provide?$q$,$q$["Delay - it primarily slows down an intruder's progress, buying time for detection and response","Detection - it automatically triggers an alarm on any contact","Response - it physically apprehends intruders","Encryption - it protects data in transit across the perimeter"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Without integrated sensors, a standard fence's core function in the deter-detect-delay-respond model is delay: it slows an intruder's progress and buys time for other controls to detect and respond, rather than detecting or responding itself.$q$),
('ci-q-1835','cissp',7,'7.14','mcq',2,$q$A facility installs continuous lighting around its parking structure that stays illuminated at the same level every night, plus separate emergency lighting on backup power in stairwells. What is the PRIMARY purpose of the emergency lighting specifically?$q$,$q$["Enabling safe evacuation during a power failure, rather than ongoing surveillance/deterrence","Increasing camera detection range at night","Reducing the facility's overall electricity costs","Replacing the need for continuous lighting entirely"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Emergency lighting activates only during a power failure and exists primarily for safe evacuation, distinct from continuous lighting's ongoing surveillance and deterrent role during normal operations.$q$),
('ci-q-1836','cissp',7,'7.14','mcq',2,$q$Why are vehicle bollards installed at a building's main entrance considered a distinct control from the property's perimeter fence?$q$,$q$["Bollards are specifically engineered/crash-rated to stop a vehicle from reaching the entrance, a threat a standard fence is not designed to resist","Bollards and fences serve an identical function and are interchangeable","Bollards are a purely decorative element with no security function","Fences are always crash-rated for vehicle impact by default"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Bollards address a specific threat - vehicle-borne intrusion or ramming - that standard perimeter fencing, designed to deter/delay pedestrian intrusion, is not engineered to resist.$q$),
('ci-q-1837','cissp',7,'7.14','mcq',2,$q$A security team needs a camera that can actively be redirected to zoom in and track a person of interest identified during a live incident, rather than passively recording one fixed area. Which camera type fits this need?$q$,$q$["A PTZ (pan-tilt-zoom) camera","A fixed camera","A CCTV monitor with no camera attached","An infrared beam sensor"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A PTZ camera can be remotely or automatically panned, tilted, and zoomed to actively track a subject, unlike a fixed camera's unchanging field of view.$q$),
('ci-q-1838','cissp',7,'7.14','mcq',1,$q$In the physical security context, is CCTV footage generally classified as a preventive or a detective control?$q$,$q$["Detective - it primarily supports identifying and investigating an event after or as it occurs, rather than physically stopping it","Preventive - the mere presence of a camera physically blocks all intrusions","Corrective - it repairs damage after an incident","Compensating - it substitutes for the need for any other physical control"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$CCTV is fundamentally detective: it records and supports investigation, but recorded footage alone doesn't physically stop an intrusion in progress without a monitored response behind it.$q$),
('ci-q-1839','cissp',7,'7.14','multi',2,$q$Select the TWO statements that correctly distinguish a proximity card from a smart card in access control.$q$,$q$["A proximity card typically broadcasts a static identifier, similar to a simple key","A smart card contains an embedded chip capable of cryptographic challenge-response authentication","A proximity card is inherently impossible to clone under any circumstances","A smart card cannot be used for physical door access, only for network login"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$A proximity card broadcasts a comparatively simple, static identifier, while a smart card's embedded chip supports cryptographic operations like challenge-response - a meaningfully stronger authentication mechanism. Neither of the other two statements is accurate: proximity cards can in fact be cloned, and smart cards are commonly used for physical door access as well as logical login.$q$),
('ci-q-1840','cissp',7,'7.14','mcq',3,$q$A high-security data center's access model requires a badge swipe at the property gate, a different badge-plus-PIN at the building entrance, a biometric scan at the server-floor door, and a physical key for an individual server cabinet. What physical security principle does this layered design most directly reflect?$q$,$q$["Concentric (layered) security zones, so that defeating any single layer does not grant access to the most sensitive asset","Single sign-on, reducing the number of credentials a legitimate user must remember","Fail-open design, prioritizing availability during a failure","The crossover error rate of the biometric scanner"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$This is a textbook concentric-zone design: each ring, from property gate to server cabinet, requires progressively stronger authentication, so defeating the outer boundary alone doesn't grant access to the most sensitive asset at the center.$q$),
('ci-q-1841','cissp',7,'7.14','mcq',2,$q$What is the primary tradeoff an organization accepts when it replaces a security guard at a badge-checked entrance with a purely automated turnstile-and-camera system?$q$,$q$["The automated system provides consistent, tireless, always-logged enforcement of the baseline rule, but loses the guard's judgment to handle exceptions or verify identity against a photo","The automated system becomes immune to all forms of social engineering by definition","The automated system is guaranteed to cost more over its lifetime than a guard","There is no meaningful tradeoff since guards and technology are functionally identical"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Technology brings consistency and tirelessness that a human can't sustain across long shifts, but it structurally cannot exercise judgment - noticing a mismatched face, sensing something is "off" - that a guard specifically provides.$q$),
('ci-q-1842','cissp',7,'7.14','ordering',3,$q$Order these physical security layers from the FIRST an intruder must overcome to the LAST, in the deter-detect-delay-respond model.$q$,$q$["Deterrence (visible lighting/signage discouraging an attempt)","Detection (CCTV/sensors identifying an attempt in progress)","Delay (fencing/bollards/locks slowing the intruder down)","Response (guards or law enforcement arriving)"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$The model runs deter, then detect, then delay, then respond: visible deterrents discourage an attempt before it starts, detection identifies an attempt already underway, delay slows the intruder's progress, and response is the arrival of guards or law enforcement before the intruder achieves their objective - each layer buying time for the next.$q$),
('ci-q-1843','cissp',7,'7.14','matching',3,$q$Match each physical security technology to the primary function it serves.$q$,$q${"left":["Access control vestibule","PTZ camera","Bollard","Smart card"],"right":["Stops a vehicle from reaching a building entrance","Proves possession of a private key via cryptographic challenge-response","Admits only one verified person per authentication cycle","Actively tracks a subject across a wider area on command"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[2,3,0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$The access control vestibule admits one verified person per cycle; a PTZ camera actively tracks a subject; a bollard stops vehicle intrusion; a smart card proves key possession via cryptographic challenge-response - four distinct physical/technical controls with distinct roles.$q$),
-- 7.15 personnel safety and security (ci-q-1844..1858)
('ci-q-1844','cissp',7,'7.15','mcq',1,$q$What is the primary purpose of a duress code entered at an access control panel?$q$,$q$["To silently signal that the person is acting under coercion, while appearing to function as normal access to anyone watching","To lock the facility down immediately and audibly for everyone nearby","To permanently disable the access panel until security resets it","To automatically call the person's personal emergency contact instead of security"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A duress code raises a silent alert to security/law enforcement while appearing to function as ordinary access, avoiding an obvious reaction that could escalate danger to the person under threat.$q$),
('ci-q-1845','cissp',7,'7.15','mcq',2,$q$Why is a duress code specifically designed to be visually and functionally indistinguishable from a normal access credential to an observer?$q$,$q$["So an attacker coercing the person cannot recognize that a silent alert was just triggered and react violently before help arrives","Because building access systems are legally required to have only one type of valid code","To reduce the manufacturing cost of the access control panel","Because indistinguishable codes are faster to enter than distinct ones"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$The indistinguishability is a safety feature - if the coercing attacker could recognize the duress code being entered, they might react violently before help ever arrives.$q$),
('ci-q-1846','cissp',7,'7.15','mcq',3,$q$An organization is preparing an executive for a business trip to a country with elevated surveillance and political risk. Which of the following is a standard security recommendation specific to this scenario?$q$,$q$["Issue a wiped loaner device with minimal data instead of the executive's normal corporate laptop and phone","Have the executive travel with their full corporate laptop, unmodified, to avoid any disruption to their workflow","Publicly announce the executive's travel itinerary in advance for transparency","Disable all communication with the home office for the duration of the trip"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A wiped loaner device with minimal data limits what an adversary can obtain even if the device is searched, imaged, or compromised while abroad - a standard mitigation for high-risk/high-surveillance travel, unlike the other choices, which each increase exposure.$q$),
('ci-q-1847','cissp',7,'7.15','mcq',2,$q$What does Kidnap and Ransom (K&R) insurance primarily cover?$q$,$q$["Negotiation consultants, ransom reimbursement, and crisis-response costs related to a kidnapping or extortion situation","General health insurance for employees traveling internationally","Lost baggage and flight cancellation costs","Cybersecurity incident response costs unrelated to personal safety"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$K&R insurance is specifically scoped to kidnapping/extortion/hostage scenarios - negotiation support, ransom reimbursement, and crisis response - not general travel or health coverage.$q$),
('ci-q-1848','cissp',7,'7.15','mcq',1,$q$In the commonly referenced Type I-IV workplace violence classification, which type describes violence committed by a customer or client the organization serves?$q$,$q$["Type II","Type I","Type III","Type IV"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Type II covers violence from a customer or client; Type I is a stranger with no legitimate business at the workplace, Type III is worker-on-worker, and Type IV is a personal relationship (e.g., domestic violence) spilling over into the workplace.$q$),
('ci-q-1849','cissp',7,'7.15','mcq',2,$q$An employee reports that a coworker has made escalating, hostile comments toward a specific colleague over several weeks. What is the appropriate next step under a mature workplace violence prevention program?$q$,$q$["Refer the report to a cross-functional threat assessment team to evaluate the actual risk level and determine an appropriate intervention","Ignore the report unless an explicit, direct threat of violence is made","Have the reporting employee confront the coworker directly to resolve it informally","Automatically terminate the coworker without any investigation"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A cross-functional threat assessment team is specifically designed to evaluate concerning-behavior reports objectively and determine risk level and intervention, rather than leaving that judgment to an untrained individual or ignoring escalating warning signs entirely.$q$),
('ci-q-1850','cissp',7,'7.15','mcq',1,$q$What is the primary function of an Employee Assistance Program (EAP) in the context of workplace violence prevention?$q$,$q$["Providing confidential counseling and support before a personal or workplace stressor escalates into a crisis","Conducting armed security patrols of the facility","Issuing duress badges to at-risk employees","Replacing the need for a threat assessment team"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$An EAP is an early, supportive intervention point - confidential counseling and support resources - intended to address stressors before they escalate, distinct from physical security patrols or the threat assessment process itself.$q$),
('ci-q-1851','cissp',7,'7.15','mcq',2,$q$A severe thunderstorm with a tornado warning is active while the building itself is undamaged and interior conditions remain safe. What is the generally appropriate response, and why?$q$,$q$["Shelter-in-place, since the threat is outside the building and moving occupants outdoors would increase their risk","Evacuation, since any active weather warning always requires leaving the building immediately","No response is required until the storm actually makes contact with the building","Immediate dispatch of all occupants to their personal vehicles in the parking lot"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Shelter-in-place is appropriate when the danger is outside and the building itself remains a safer location than the outdoors - the opposite logic from an internal hazard like a fire, which calls for evacuation instead.$q$),
('ci-q-1852','cissp',7,'7.15','mcq',1,$q$A fire alarm activates and smoke is visible in a building's main corridor. What is the generally appropriate response, and why?$q$,$q$["Evacuation, since the hazard is inside the building and remaining indoors increases the danger","Shelter-in-place, since fires are always safer to wait out indoors","Immediate lockdown with all doors sealed shut","No action until the fire department physically arrives on scene"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Evacuation is appropriate when the threat is inside the building itself, since remaining indoors would expose occupants to greater danger than leaving - the opposite scenario from an external hazard like severe weather.$q$),
('ci-q-1853','cissp',7,'7.15','mcq',2,$q$What is the primary responsibility of a designated floor/evacuation warden during a building evacuation?$q$,$q$["Confirming their assigned area is fully cleared, assisting anyone needing help, and reporting status to incident command","Operating the fire suppression system directly","Serving as the sole point of contact with media during the incident","Approving the organization's disaster recovery budget"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A floor warden provides accountability during an evacuation - confirming their zone is actually clear, assisting anyone needing help, and reporting status - going well beyond simply "the alarm sounded."$q$),
('ci-q-1854','cissp',7,'7.15','mcq',1,$q$What is the primary purpose of a designated assembly point (muster point) after a building evacuation?$q$,$q$["Enabling a headcount against expected occupancy so responders can determine whether anyone is still unaccounted for","Providing a place for employees to resume working on laptops during the evacuation","Serving as the location where the fire department extinguishes the fire","Replacing the need for a fire alarm system"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A muster point exists so a headcount can confirm everyone actually got out - without it, an evacuation provides no verifiable way to know whether someone is still unaccounted for and possibly still inside.$q$),
('ci-q-1855','cissp',7,'7.15','multi',2,$q$Select the TWO statements that correctly describe sound personnel-safety practice for evacuation and shelter-in-place decisions.$q$,$q$["Evacuation is generally appropriate when the hazard is inside the building or the building itself is unsafe","Shelter-in-place is generally appropriate when the danger is outside and moving people outdoors would increase risk","A muster point and headcount are unnecessary as long as the fire alarm sounded","Floor wardens exist only to operate elevators during a fire"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$The choice between evacuation and shelter-in-place hinges on where the actual danger is located; a muster point and headcount remain necessary regardless of whether the alarm sounded, and floor wardens are responsible for confirming areas are clear and assisting occupants, not operating elevators.$q$),
('ci-q-1856','cissp',7,'7.15','mcq',3,$q$A security team wants to reduce a traveling executive's exposure without eliminating necessary international travel entirely. Which combination of controls most directly targets executive-specific travel security risk, as opposed to general employee travel risk?$q$,$q$["A pre-travel risk assessment, a wiped loaner device, and Kidnap and Ransom (K&R) insurance","A stronger password policy applied company-wide","A general security awareness training video shown to all new hires","An annual penetration test of the corporate website"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Pre-travel risk assessments, loaner devices, and K&R insurance are targeted, elevated controls specifically suited to higher-risk executive travel, unlike company-wide password policy, generic new-hire training, or a website penetration test, none of which address personal travel safety.$q$),
('ci-q-1857','cissp',7,'7.15','mcq',2,$q$Why does the principle of "life safety first" mean a fail-safe (fail-open) electronic lock is generally preferred over a fail-secure lock on an emergency fire-exit door?$q$,$q$["Life safety takes precedence over asset protection - unlocking during a power/fire failure lets occupants evacuate, even at the cost of temporarily reduced access security","Fail-secure locks are always more expensive to install than fail-safe locks","Fire codes have no bearing on how emergency-exit locks should behave","Fail-open locks eliminate the need for any evacuation planning"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Life safety takes precedence over asset protection in personnel-safety planning; a fail-safe door unlocks during a failure so people can evacuate, accepting a temporary security tradeoff rather than trapping occupants behind a still-locked fail-secure door.$q$),
('ci-q-1858','cissp',7,'7.15','ordering',3,$q$Order the following personnel-safety response steps for a workplace violence threat report, from the first appropriate action to the last.$q$,$q$["An employee or manager reports concerning behavior through the established reporting channel","The threat assessment team evaluates the report and determines the risk level","An appropriate intervention is selected and implemented (EAP referral, security involvement, disciplinary action, or law enforcement)","The outcome and any near-miss lessons are documented to improve the prevention program"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$The process runs report, then assessment, then intervention, then documentation - each step depends on the one before it, and skipping straight to intervention without an objective risk assessment risks either over- or under-reacting to the reported behavior.$q$);
